1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,440
The last section of this chapter deals with the prostate.

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:05,200
A few basic principles before we move forward.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:08,740
Of course, the prostate is a small round gland,

4
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:11,540
and it sits at the base of the bladder.

5
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:14,160
So here's the bladder, here's the prostate.

6
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:16,260
Running through the center of the prostate

7
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:17,780
is the urethra.

8
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:20,260
We call that the prostatic urethra.

9
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:23,740
And then the prostate sits anterior to the rectum.

10
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:24,900
And of course, that's important

11
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:26,920
because when we do a digital rectal exam,

12
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:31,340
we're able to palpate the posterior aspect of the prostate.

13
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:36,920
Histologically, the prostate consists of both glands and stroma.

14
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:39,060
The stroma represents the connective tissue,

15
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:42,760
and the glands represent the functional unit of the prostate.

16
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:46,480
Remember that the prostate secretes an alkaline milky liquid

17
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:50,160
that is added to the sperm and seminovesical fluid.

18
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:52,760
The glands of the prostate actually contain two layers.

19
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:56,620
There's a inner luminal layer and an outer basal cell layer.

20
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:59,300
And overall, both the glands and the stroma

21
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:01,900
are maintained by androgens.

22
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:03,940
The first disorder is acute prostatitis.

23
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:05,920
This is acute inflammation of the prostate,

24
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:07,780
usually due to bacteria.

25
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:09,160
The bacteria that are involved here

26
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:11,500
and the demographics are exactly the same

27
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:14,180
as those that we discussed in orchitis.

28
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:17,260
So that in young adults, chlamydia and neisseria

29
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:20,680
are the most common causes of acute prostatitis.

30
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:23,280
Whereas in older adults, the organisms that are usually

31
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:27,960
responsible for urinary tract infection, such as E. coli and pseudomonas,

32
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:32,040
are also the most important causes of acute prostatitis.

33
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:35,820
Patients with acute prostatitis present with pain during urination,

34
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:38,460
along with fever and chills.

35
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:41,800
On digital rectal exam, the prostate would be tender and boggy.

36
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:43,620
That's particularly high yield.

37
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:46,520
And the prostatic secretions would show white blood cells,

38
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:49,880
with the culture usually revealing bacteria.

39
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:53,260
Chronic prostatitis is chronic inflammation of the prostate.

40
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:54,840
It also presents with dysuria.

41
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:58,760
However, this is usually associated with pelvic or low back pain.

42
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:02,380
Again, here the prostatic secretions would also show white blood cells.

43
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:06,040
However, the cultures are usually negative.

44
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:08,400
BPH is benign prostatic hyperplasia.

45
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:12,060
This is hyperplasia of both the prostatic stroma,

46
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:13,780
as well as the glands.

47
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:15,380
It's an age-related change.

48
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:17,880
It's seen in most men by the age of 50.

49
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:21,680
Important to note that despite the fact that we call this hyperplasia,

50
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:24,100
there is no increased risk for cancer.

51
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:26,840
Now, I am particular about pointing this out because,

52
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:29,020
generally speaking, recall from chapter one,

53
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:31,380
that when you have pathologic hyperplasia,

54
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:36,640
that's classically a soil or a risk factor for the development of cancer.

55
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:39,640
However, in the case of benign prostatic hyperplasia,

56
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:42,440
there is no increased risk for cancer.

57
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:44,180
Very important to note this.

58
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:46,940
BPH is driven by DHT.

59
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:52,400
Recall that testosterone is converted to DHT by 5-alpha reductase in the stromal cells.

60
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:58,700
That DHT then acts on the androgen receptors of both the stromal and epithelial cells

61
00:02:58,700 --> 00:03:01,120
to result in hyperplastic nodules.

62
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:03,560
It's important to note, and particularly high yield,

63
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:07,520
that BPH occurs in the periurethral zone of the prostate.

64
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:12,280
So that if I took the prostate on cross-section and the urethra is running through the center of it,

65
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,740
BPH occurs here, around the periurethral zone.

66
00:03:15,740 --> 00:03:21,280
Now, that's particularly important because BPH will then result in compression of the urethra,

67
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:27,200
and most of the clinical features of BPH are all due to urinary obstruction.

68
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:32,120
For example, patients will have problems starting and stopping the urine stream.

69
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:34,960
Patients also experience impaired bladder emptying.

70
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:39,580
Again, recall that the prostate sits at the base of the bladder,

71
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:43,420
and running through the prostate, of course, is the urethra.

72
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:46,960
Now, BPH is occurring in the periurethral zone,

73
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:51,480
which is then obstructing the, which is then creating an obstruction,

74
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:54,260
and this will impair bladder emptying.

75
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:57,720
In addition, the bladder is going to have to press against that obstruction,

76
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:02,900
and so therefore the smooth muscle of the wall of the bladder will undergo hypertrophy.

77
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:07,720
It's also possible that the high pressure generated by the bladder as it pushes against the obstruction

78
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:12,100
could result in diverticuli, or outpouching, of the bladder wall.

79
00:04:12,100 --> 00:04:18,400
So these are all, again, consequences of the fact that BPH occurs in the periurethral zone.

80
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:22,560
Patients can also experience dribbling, hypertrophy of the bladder wall smooth muscle,

81
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:26,020
as I've already stated, microscopic hematuria may be present,

82
00:04:26,020 --> 00:04:31,020
and important to note that the PSA is often slightly elevated.

83
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:36,020
Now, the reason why the PSA is elevated is because PSA is produced by the prostatic glands,

84
00:04:36,020 --> 00:04:39,700
and so if there are extra prostatic glands because there is hyperplasia,

85
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:42,160
there will be an increase in PSA as well.

86
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:45,940
But at the same time, it's important to note that this is only a slight elevation,

87
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:49,400
so that the normal PSA ranges from 0 to 4.

88
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:54,580
Generally speaking, in BPH, the PSA will range from anywhere from 4 to 10,

89
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:58,680
and this is considered a slight elevation in the PSA.

90
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:02,400
One of the complications of obstruction of the bladder is that you can also have subsequent

91
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:07,740
buildup of fluid backing up into the kidney, and when fluid backs up into the kidney because

92
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:10,740
it can't be drained, that's called hydronephrosis.

93
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:15,940
Of course, hydronephrosis results in dilatation of the ureter and can also result in dilatation

94
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:20,840
and atrophy of the kidney, and so this is an image of hydronephrosis.

95
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:23,460
There are multiple possible treatments for BPH.

96
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:29,280
One possibility would be to use alpha-1 antagonists, and this again would relax smooth muscle.

97
00:05:29,280 --> 00:05:33,420
Now, the benefit of alpha-1 antagonists such as teresosin is not only do they relax the

98
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:38,880
smooth muscle to then relieve the obstruction of the prostate, but in addition, teresosin would

99
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:43,140
benefit the patient by relaxing the smooth muscle of the blood vessel wall, so that if

100
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:47,020
the patients are hypertensive, you sort of kill two birds with one stone.

101
00:05:47,020 --> 00:05:52,640
If the patient happens to be normal-tensive, then a selective alpha-1A antagonist could

102
00:05:52,640 --> 00:06:00,820
be used, such as tamulosin, which would avoid the alpha-1B effects on the vascular smooth muscle.

103
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:06,180
An additional pharmacologic treatment would be to use a 5-alpha reductase inhibitor.

104
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:12,000
Now recall that BPH is driven by DHT, and it's 5-alpha reductase that converts testosterone

105
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,380
to DHT.

106
00:06:13,380 --> 00:06:17,680
Now the unfortunate thing about 5-alpha reductase inhibitors is that they usually take months

107
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:20,140
to produce results.

108
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:23,560
Prostatic adenocarcinoma is the last topic that we're going to discuss.

109
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:27,180
Again, adeno means gland, and carcinoma means malignant.

110
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:32,320
So this is a malignant proliferation of glands, of course involving the prostate.

111
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:36,240
Prostatic adenocarcinoma is the most common cancer in men, and it's the second most common

112
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:38,220
cause of cancer death.

113
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:39,520
Risk factors include age.

114
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:43,700
As a person ages, there's an increased risk for prostatic adenocarcinoma.

115
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:44,900
Race.

116
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:49,200
Prostatic adenocarcinoma has a higher incidence in African Americans.

117
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:54,240
Slightly beneath that is the incidence in Caucasians, and then slightly less than that is the incidence

118
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:55,700
in Asians.

119
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:59,980
So it's relatively more common in African Americans and less common in Asians.

120
00:06:59,980 --> 00:07:04,700
And another risk factor is diets that are high in saturated fats.

121
00:07:04,700 --> 00:07:06,820
Here's a cross section of the prostate.

122
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:09,680
Notice that the urethra runs through the center of the prostate.

123
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:13,700
Now it's important to note that prostatic adenocarcinoma, which is the lesion that I'm circling

124
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:19,020
here, occurs in the posterior periphery of the prostate.

125
00:07:19,020 --> 00:07:25,300
Now the most common location for prostatic adenocarcinoma, again, is in the posterior periphery of the

126
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:26,300
prostate.

127
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:30,340
Now that's important because notice that it's very far away from the periurethra zone.

128
00:07:30,340 --> 00:07:35,700
And so therefore, prostatic adenocarcinoma usually does not produce urinary symptoms

129
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:37,340
until very late.

130
00:07:37,340 --> 00:07:42,300
Only if the cancer were to grow extensively and actually compress the periurethra zone would

131
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:44,000
you get urinary symptoms.

132
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:49,340
So generally speaking, prostatic adenocarcinoma is actually clinically silent and no urinary

133
00:07:49,340 --> 00:07:51,240
symptoms are usually present.

134
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:55,000
Therefore, in order to detect prostate cancer, we need to screen.

135
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,820
And so screening begins at the age of 50 with both digital rectal exam and PSA.

136
00:07:59,820 --> 00:08:04,460
Now recall that if this is the prostate here and it sits anterior to the rectum, then the

137
00:08:04,460 --> 00:08:09,180
digital rectal exam is actually analyzing the exact location in which prostate cancer usually

138
00:08:09,180 --> 00:08:10,600
occurs.

139
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:15,360
Because remember that prostate cancer usually arises in the posterior periphery of the

140
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:16,820
prostate.

141
00:08:16,820 --> 00:08:19,520
An additional method of screening is the PSA.

142
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:25,040
The normal PSA ranges from anywhere from 0 to 4 nanograms per deciliter.

143
00:08:25,040 --> 00:08:30,840
Now as patients age, that normal PSA can increase such that the range from 4 to 10 is sort of

144
00:08:30,840 --> 00:08:36,100
a borderline number in which depending on the age, it may or may not be elevated for a particular

145
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:37,100
patient.

146
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:41,860
However, once the PSA gets to be above 10, that's highly worrisome at any age.

147
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:46,920
And so that would then prompt us to become particularly worried about prostate cancer.

148
00:08:46,920 --> 00:08:51,020
Another way by which we can screen is to look at the free PSA in the serum.

149
00:08:51,020 --> 00:08:55,860
Now generally speaking, cancer makes bound PSA so that a decrease in the percentage of free

150
00:08:55,860 --> 00:08:59,060
PSA would be suggestive of cancer.

151
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:03,820
Now when the clinical suspicion is sufficient, then a prostatic biopsy will be performed.

152
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:09,400
And it's important to note that in order to make a diagnosis of cancer, this is required.

153
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:14,620
You cannot assume that the patient has cancer based on a high PSA or an abnormal digital

154
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:15,820
rectal exam.

155
00:09:15,820 --> 00:09:20,580
So prostatic biopsy must be performed before the prostate would ever be removed.

156
00:09:20,580 --> 00:09:27,040
On biopsy, we would classically see small glands that infiltrate the normal prostatic tissue.

157
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:32,580
And one of the high yield characteristics of prostate cancer is that the nuclei, which I'm

158
00:09:32,580 --> 00:09:35,580
outlining here, contain these dark nucleoli.

159
00:09:35,580 --> 00:09:40,280
Again, the nuclei contain these dark nucleoli.

160
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:44,540
And that's particularly characteristic for prostatic carcinoma.

161
00:09:44,540 --> 00:09:48,420
The grading of prostate cancer occurs via the Gleason grading system.

162
00:09:48,420 --> 00:09:53,120
Very high yield to note that the Gleason grading system is based on architecture alone.

163
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:57,860
And that nuclear atypia is not considered in the Gleason grading system.

164
00:09:57,860 --> 00:10:01,640
In order to perform the grading, multiple regions of the tumor are assessed.

165
00:10:01,640 --> 00:10:05,340
Now the reason for this is that there is high variability in the architecture of prostate cancer.

166
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:07,900
Or prostate cancer across the tumor.

167
00:10:07,900 --> 00:10:11,620
So we actually have to look at multiple regions and then score different regions in order to

168
00:10:11,620 --> 00:10:13,420
come up with a final score.

169
00:10:13,420 --> 00:10:17,500
And this is classically done by looking at the two most common patterns that exist within

170
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:18,620
the cancer.

171
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:21,500
Those patterns are scored anywhere from one to five.

172
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:25,580
And then the two scores are added to produce a final score, which would range from two to

173
00:10:25,580 --> 00:10:26,580
ten.

174
00:10:26,580 --> 00:10:28,780
The higher the score, the worse the prognosis.

175
00:10:28,780 --> 00:10:32,980
But again, the highest yield here is that the Gleason grading is based on architecture

176
00:10:32,980 --> 00:10:35,480
and not nuclear atypia.

177
00:10:35,480 --> 00:10:39,540
One of the important things about prostate cancer is that it often likes to spread to

178
00:10:39,540 --> 00:10:40,540
the lumbar spine.

179
00:10:40,540 --> 00:10:46,760
When it spreads to the lumbar spine, it produces osteoblastic metastases such that rather than

180
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:51,420
getting lytic punched out lesions, you get these sclerotic lesions of the bone.

181
00:10:51,420 --> 00:10:56,140
Now this would present with low back pain and the alkaline phosphatase would be increased.

182
00:10:56,140 --> 00:11:00,980
Again alkaline phosphatase is a sign of osteoblastic activity indicating that bone is being laid

183
00:11:00,980 --> 00:11:01,980
down.

184
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:06,740
And we're well aware of the fact that we get osteoblastic metastases with prostate cancer.

185
00:11:06,740 --> 00:11:12,880
In addition, we might see an elevation of PSA or the prostatic acid phosphatase, both of

186
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:16,160
which are serum tumor markers for prostate cancer.

187
00:11:16,160 --> 00:11:20,420
Here's a picture of the osteoblastic metastases involving the lumbar spine.

188
00:11:20,420 --> 00:11:24,720
And so you can see this area of sclerosis within the spine.

189
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:29,060
With local disease, prostate cancer is treated with prostatectomy.

190
00:11:29,060 --> 00:11:30,980
So that is removal of the prostate.

191
00:11:30,980 --> 00:11:37,020
If there is advanced disease, continuous GNRH analogs such as leuprolide can be used.

192
00:11:37,020 --> 00:11:42,140
Now the idea here is that the GNRH analogs, when continuous, they shut down the hypothalamus,

193
00:11:42,140 --> 00:11:47,640
which then reduces luteinizing hormone and FSH, which then in turn decrease the ability

194
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:53,060
of the prostate cancer to thrive because of course prostate cancer is androgen dependent.

195
00:11:53,060 --> 00:11:58,300
In addition, we can use androgen receptor inhibitors such as flutamide, and again the notion here

196
00:11:58,300 --> 00:12:03,760
is that prostate cancer is dependent on androgens in order to grow and thrive.

197
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:05,980
And that concludes both the section and this chapter.

198
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:07,100
Okay.

199
00:12:07,100 --> 00:12:07,420
Thank you.

200
00:12:07,420 --> 00:12:11,320
We'll see you next time.

201
00:12:11,320 --> 00:12:21,360
Now let's see you next time.

